<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claude of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1499">1499</a>–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claude of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was the eldest daughter of Louis XII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>, and from 1514 duchess of Brittany. Married in 1514 to Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> in 1515, she was queen of France until her death in 1524. She granted her husband the administration of Brittany, which passed to their son the Dauphin Francis and was united with France in 1532.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claude-of-france.jpg" alt="Claude of France" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Jean Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_de_France_(1499-1524).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Claude was born on 13 October 1499 at Romorantin-Lanthenay and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, a saint her mother had invoked on a pilgrimage so that she might bear a living child. Of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>'s at least fourteen pregnancies, only Claude and her sister Renée, born in 1510, survived to adulthood. Because Anne had no surviving sons, Claude was heir presumptive to Brittany, while the French crown could pass only through male heirs under Salic Law.</p><p>Queen Anne sought to keep Brittany separate from the crown by marrying Claude to the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and the contract was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> on 10 August 1501. The first Treaty of Blois of 1504 gave Claude a large dowry if her father died without male heirs, including Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Blois and Asti. In 1505 the ailing Louis XII cancelled the engagement at the Estates General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, after Louise of Savoy had obtained a secret promise that Claude would marry her son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Claude became Duchess of Brittany on 9 January 1514, when her mother died, and on 18 May, at fourteen, she married her cousin Francis. Louis XII died on 1 January 1515, less than three months after marrying Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and Francis and Claude became king and queen. She was crowned at the Basilica of St Denis on 10 May 1517.</p><p>She never ruled Brittany: in 1515 she gave its government to her husband in perpetuity. She refused his repeated suggestion that Brittany be incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and named her eldest son, Francis, heir to the duchy. At court she was eclipsed by her mother-in-law, Louise of Savoy, and had little interest in politics, preferring religion. Anne Boleyn was in her household until late 1521. She spent almost all her marriage in annual pregnancies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Claude died on 26 July 1524 at the Château de Blois, aged twenty-four, and was buried at St Denis. Sources disagree on the cause: childbirth or miscarriage, exhaustion after many pregnancies, or bone tuberculosis, as her mother had. Her eldest son, Francis, succeeded her as Duke of Brittany, with his father as guardian, and after his death in 1536 her second son, Henry, became Dauphin and Duke of Brittany. He later became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as Henry II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Claude of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Romorantin-Lanthenay, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Blois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.36,1.74&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Romorantin-Lanthenay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4591 503 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Claude of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5041 773h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5041 773h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.36,1.74&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
